  "description": "Method replication: the four photographs are the paper's own pictures of its four lampposts, but participants move between them on screen instead of walking between the real lights in a park after dark. Four outdoor lighting applications on lampposts numbered 1-4 along the path of a small formal garden in a municipal park in Malmö (1 clear ceramic metal halide, 2 frosted ceramic metal halide, 3 LED 3912 K, 4 LED 4051 K). Participants first see all four lampposts in order, then follow the paper's random environmental walking procedure with an on-screen four-sided die: a first throw sets the starting lamp; then, 40 times, the die is thrown again and the participant either walks to the lamp it shows if they prefer it to their current one (or stays, if it shows their current lamp), or throws again and must go to whichever lamp the second throw shows. Preference is the number of times each lamp is walked to. Age and gender are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Patching, Rahm, Jansson & Johansson (2017), A New Method of Random Environmental Walking for Assessing Behavioral Preferences for Different Lighting Applications, Frontiers in Psychology 8: 345, with the photographs cropped from its Figure 2 and the procedure steps from its text (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: L1-L4 (lamppost numbers). Each step is stored under walk (lamp after the step, keyed s01-s40) and decision (accept, stay or rethrow); start holds the first lamp. Analysis as in the paper: count the walks to each lamp per participant and compare lamps with a mixed model (participant random intercept) or chi-square; in the field test (80 adults) the rank order of walks was 3, 4, 2, 1, and walk counts were related to the perceived pleasantness and perceived comfort quality ratings. Differences from the original study: (1) photographs on a screen instead of the lit garden at night, and the photographs are not dark night views, so the test reflects the look of each lamppost and its setting rather than the light at night; (2) the Perceived Outdoor Lighting Quality scale, the perceived flicker rating and the eight-item pleasantness semantic differential are not included because the paper does not print their items; (3) the die is simulated on screen, and the instruction texts are ours, built from the paper's step-by-step procedure; (4) the age and gender questions are ours.",
